DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     VVNNEETT is the name of a technique to virtualize the network stack.  The ba‐
     sic idea is to change global resources most notably variables into per
     network stack resources and have functions, sysctls, eventhandlers, etc.
     access and handle them in the context of the correct instance.  Each
     (virtual) network stack is attached to a _p_r_i_s_o_n, with _v_n_e_t_0 being the un‐
     restricted default network stack of the base system.

     The global defines for VNET_SETNAME and VNET_SYMPREFIX are shared with
     kvm(3) to access internals for debugging reasons.

   VVaarriiaabbllee DDeeccllaarraattiioonn
     Variables are virtualized by using the VVNNEETT__DDEEFFIINNEE() macro rather than
     writing them out as _t_y_p_e _n_a_m_e.  One can still use static initialization,
     e.g.,

           VNET_DEFINE(int, foo) = 1;

     Variables declared with the static keyword can use the
     VVNNEETT__DDEEFFIINNEE__SSTTAATTIICC() macro, e.g.,

           VNET_DEFINE_STATIC(SLIST_HEAD(, bar), bars);

     Static initialization is not possible when the virtualized variable would
     need to be referenced, e.g., with “TAILQ_HEAD_INITIALIZER()”.  In that
     case a VVNNEETT__SSYYSSIINNIITT() based initialization function must be used.

     External variables have to be declared using the VVNNEETT__DDEECCLLAARREE() macro.
     In either case the convention is to define another macro, that is then
     used throughout the implementation to access that variable.  The variable
     name is usually prefixed by _V__ to express that it is virtualized.  The
     VVNNEETT() macro will then translate accesses to that variable to the copy of
     the currently selected instance (see the _V_i_r_t_u_a_l _i_n_s_t_a_n_c_e _s_e_l_e_c_t_i_o_n sec‐
     tion):

           #define   V_name    VNET(name)

     _N_O_T_E_: Do not confuse this with the convention used by VFS(9).

     The VVNNEETT__NNAAMMEE() macro returns the offset within the memory region of the
     virtual network stack instance.  It is usually only used with
     SSYYSSCCTTLL__VVNNEETT__**() macros.

   VViirrttuuaall IInnssttaannccee SSeelleeccttiioonn
     There are three different places where the current virtual network stack
     pointer is stored and can be taken from:

           1.   a _p_r_i_s_o_n:
                      (struct prison *)->pr_vnet

                For convenience the following macros are provided:
                      CCRREEDD__TTOO__VVNNEETT(_s_t_r_u_c_t _u_c_r_e_d _*)
                      TTDD__TTOO__VVNNEETT(_s_t_r_u_c_t _t_h_r_e_a_d _*)
                      PP__TTOO__VVNNEETT(_s_t_r_u_c_t _p_r_o_c _*)

           2.   a _s_o_c_k_e_t:
                      (struct socket *)->so_vnet

           3.   an _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e:
                      (struct ifnet *)->if_vnet

     In addition the currently active instance is cached in
     “curthread->td_vnet” which is usually only accessed through the curvnet
     macro.

     To set the correct context of the current virtual network instance, use
     the CCUURRVVNNEETT__SSEETT() or CCUURRVVNNEETT__SSEETT__QQUUIIEETT() macros.  The CCUURRVVNNEETT__SSEETT__QQUUIIEETT()
     version will not record vnet recursions in case the kernel was compiled
     with ooppttiioonnss VVNNEETT__DDEEBBUUGG and should thus only be used in well known cases,
     where recursion is unavoidable.  Both macros will save the previous state
     on the stack and it must be restored with the CCUURRVVNNEETT__RREESSTTOORREE() macro.

     _N_O_T_E_: As the previous state is saved on the stack, you cannot have multi‐
     ple CCUURRVVNNEETT__SSEETT() calls in the same block.

     _N_O_T_E_: As the previous state is saved on the stack, a CCUURRVVNNEETT__RREESSTTOORREE()
     call has to be in the same block as the CCUURRVVNNEETT__SSEETT() call or in a sub‐
     block with the same idea of the saved instances as the outer block.

     _N_O_T_E_: As each macro is a set of operations and, as previously explained,
     cannot be put into its own block when defined, one cannot conditionally
     set the current vnet context.  The following will _n_o_t work:

           if (condition)
                   CURVNET_SET(vnet);

     nor would this work:

           if (condition) {
                   CURVNET_SET(vnet);
           }
           CURVNET_RESTORE();

     Sometimes one needs to loop over all virtual instances, for example to
     update virtual from global state, to run a function from a callout(9) for
     each instance, etc.  For those cases the VVNNEETT__IITTEERRAATTOORR__DDEECCLL() and
     VVNNEETT__FFOORREEAACCHH() macros are provided.  The former macro defines the vari‐
     able that iterates over the loop, and the latter loops over all of the
     virtual network stack instances.  See _L_o_c_k_i_n_g for how to savely traverse
     the list of all virtual instances.

     The IISS__DDEEFFAAUULLTT__VVNNEETT() macro provides a safe way to check whether the cur‐
     rently active instance is the unrestricted default network stack of the
     base system (_v_n_e_t_0).

     The VVNNEETT__AASSSSEERRTT() macro provides a way to conditionally add assertions
     that are only active with ooppttiioonnss VVIIMMAAGGEE compiled in and either ooppttiioonnss
     VVNNEETT__DDEEBBUUGG or ooppttiioonnss IINNVVAARRIIAANNTTSS enabled as well.  It uses the same se‐
     mantics as KASSERT(9).

   LLoocckkiinngg
     For public access to the list of virtual network stack instances e.g., by
     the VVNNEETT__FFOORREEAACCHH() macro, read locks are provided.  Macros are used to
     abstract from the actual type of the locks.  If a caller may sleep while
     traversing the list, it must use the VVNNEETT__LLIISSTT__RRLLOOCCKK() and
     VVNNEETT__LLIISSTT__RRUUNNLLOOCCKK() macros.  Otherwise, the caller can use
     VVNNEETT__LLIISSTT__RRLLOOCCKK__NNOOSSLLEEEEPP() and VVNNEETT__LLIISSTT__RRUUNNLLOOCCKK__NNOOSSLLEEEEPP().

   SSttaarrttuupp aanndd TTeeaarrddoowwnn FFuunnccttiioonnss
     To start or tear down a virtual network stack instance the internal func‐
     tions vvnneett__aalllloocc() and vvnneett__ddeessttrrooyy() are provided and called from the
     jail framework.  They run the publicly provided methods to handle network
     stack startup and teardown.

     For public control, the system startup interface has been enhanced to not
     only handle a system boot but to also handle a virtual network stack
     startup and teardown.  To the base system the VVNNEETT__SSYYSSIINNIITT() and
     VVNNEETT__SSYYSSUUNNIINNIITT() macros look exactly as if there were no virtual network
     stack.  In fact, if ooppttiioonnss VVIIMMAAGGEE is not compiled in they are compiled
     to the standard SSYYSSIINNIITT() macros.  In addition to that they are run for
     each virtual network stack when starting or, in reverse order, when shut‐
     ting down.

   EEvveenntthhaannddlleerrss
     Eventhandlers can be handled in two ways:

           1.   save the _t_a_g_s returned in each virtual instance and properly
                free the eventhandlers on teardown using those, or
           2.   use one eventhandler that will iterate over all virtual net‐
                work stack instances.

     For the first case one can just use the normal EVENTHANDLER(9) functions,
     while for the second case the VVNNEETT__GGLLOOBBAALL__EEVVEENNTTHHAANNDDLLEERR__RREEGGIISSTTEERR() and
     VVNNEETT__GGLLOOBBAALL__EEVVEENNTTHHAANNDDLLEERR__RREEGGIISSTTEERR__TTAAGG() macros are provided.  These dif‐
     fer in that VVNNEETT__GGLLOOBBAALL__EEVVEENNTTHHAANNDDLLEERR__RREEGGIISSTTEERR__TTAAGG() takes an extra first
     argument that will carry the _t_a_g upon return.  Eventhandlers registered
     with either of these will not run _f_u_n_c directly but _f_u_n_c will be called
     from an internal iterator function for each vnet.  Both macros can only
     be used for eventhandlers that do not take additional arguments, as the
     variadic arguments from an EVENTHANDLER_INVOKE(9) call will be ignored.

   SSyyssccttll HHaannddlliinngg
     A sysctl(9) can be virtualized by using one of the SSYYSSCCTTLL__VVNNEETT__**()
     macros.

     They take the same arguments as the standard sysctl(9) functions, with
     the only difference, that the _p_t_r argument has to be passed as
     ‘&VNET_NAME(foo)’ instead of ‘&foo’ so that the variable can be selected
     from the correct memory region of the virtual network stack instance of
     the caller.

     For the very rare case a sysctl handler function would want to handle
     _a_r_g_1 itself the VVNNEETT__SSYYSSCCTTLL__AARRGG(_r_e_q, _a_r_g_1) is provided that will trans‐
     late the _a_r_g_1 argument to the correct memory address in the virtual net‐
     work stack context of the caller.

